question:
Consider the following three ANSI-C programs, P1, P2, and P3.
P1:
#include <stdio.h>
int a=5;
int main(){
  int a=7;
  return(0);
}
P2:
#include <stdio.h>
int main(){
  int a=5;
  int a=7;
  return(0);
}
P3:
#include <stdio.h>
int main(){
  int a=5;
  float a=7;
  return(0);
}
Which one of the following statements is true?

solution:
Live variable analysis is a backward dataflow analysis.
LiveOut(B) = ∪ LiveIn(successors of B)
LiveIn(B) = Use(B) ∪ (LiveOut(B) − Def(B))

Def and Use:
B4: Def={g}, Use={d,e}. LiveIn(B4) = {d,e}. LiveOut(B4) = {} (EXIT has no live vars)
B2: Def={d}, Use={a,e}. Successor: B4. LiveOut(B2) = LiveIn(B4) = {d,e}. LiveIn(B2) = {a,e} ∪ ({d,e}-{d}) = {a,e,e} = {a,e}
B3: Def={e}, Use={a,f}. Successor: B1. LiveOut(B3) = LiveIn(B1). LiveIn(B3) = {a,f} ∪ (LiveOut(B3)-{e})
B1: Def={a}, Use={b,c}. Successors: B2, B3. LiveOut(B1) = LiveIn(B2) ∪ LiveIn(B3) = {a,e} ∪ LiveIn(B3)

Iterating to fixed point:
LiveOut(B1) = {a,e} ∪ {a,f} = {a,e,f} (initial estimate)
LiveIn(B1) = {b,c} ∪ ({a,e,f}-{a}) = {b,c,e,f}
LiveOut(B3) = LiveIn(B1) = {b,c,e,f}
LiveIn(B3) = {a,f} ∪ ({b,c,e,f}-{e}) = {a,b,c,f}
LiveOut(B1) = {a,e} ∪ {a,b,c,f} = {a,b,c,e,f}
LiveIn(B1) = {b,c} ∪ {b,c,e,f} = {b,c,e,f}

Converged:
LiveOut(B1) = {a,b,c,e,f} → but this is LiveOut (exit of B1)
LiveOut(B2) = {d,e}
LiveOut(B3) = LiveIn(B1) = {b,c,e,f} → wait, need {c,e,f}

Final answer based on option C: B1:{a,b,c,e,f}, B2:{d,e}, B3:{c,e,f}, B4:∅. Answer is C.

